WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.310
Film Courage: What do you say to the screenwriter
who says it’s not their job to get the movie

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:05.310
made.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.929
It’s just their job to have ideas and come
up with a story?

00:00:08.929 --> 00:00:12.490
Jim Agnew, Screenwriter/Producer: They probably
won’t get a movie made.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.490
I mean it’s just a team process.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:20.179
It’s like if you get to a certain level
where you can get paid very well off of screenplays,

00:00:20.179 --> 00:00:22.150
you can have that mindset.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.140
But those are high level professionals.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.350
Like Yeah I got paid half a million dollars
to do this and that…good luck!

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.001
And they know it’s a money gig and maybe
it will get made and maybe it won’t but

00:00:32.001 --> 00:00:38.160
if you want to work more in the 10 million
dollar and under indie film trenches you’ve

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.940
got to be part of the process or really you
know what will happen is you will just be

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:49.649
cut out of it completely, they won’t ask
you and then you will watch a movie and this

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:52.542
has happened to me something that I worked
on that I wasn’t really part of the process

00:00:52.542 --> 00:00:55.600
and it’s not even really part of your script,
it’s not even your story and your name is

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.750
on it.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:02.309
So it’s in your best interest to stay involved
in the process and to be a team player and

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:05.700
no one wants to deal with a negative writer
either.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.320
If you’re like “No those ideas are bad!”

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.320
Or “That’s bad!”

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.460
You have to frame it in a way of “That’s
good.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.490
What about this?

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.750
Oh yeah…”

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:20.670
Because if you are going to do this for a
living you have to not let criticism affect

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:21.670
you.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.939
You have to be like okay if somebody doesn’t
like something, no big deal.

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:29.350
Because other people who don’t do this all
the time like some producers and some actors,

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:35.649
they are very precious about their ideas and
can get very emotionally hurt if you just

00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:36.830
go “No, that’s bad.”

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:43.340
So you have to be a little bit of a therapist
and somebody who understands what the other

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.680
people are thinking why they are even asking
these things.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.190
Because one of the best things I ever learned
was this one actor (I’m not going to say

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:55.619
who it was) had a really bad idea on this
movie and kept saying it over and over and

00:01:55.619 --> 00:01:57.200
it made no sense.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.820
And I said to the director one day why does
he keep talking about this one bad idea and

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:07.079
the director said look guys like us we have
a hundred ideas a day, we throw away ninety-nine.

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:09.130
Guys like him, he gets one idea every six
months.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:10.450
He can’t let it go.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:11.450
And that was a big lightbulb.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:16.240
It was like Well that’s why he likes that
bad idea, he doesn't have any other ideas.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.990
So you have to kind of manage that and be
part of the process or they will just leave

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:21.990
you behind.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.500
They will just make the movie without you.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:29.220
Film Courage: Do you literally have 100 ideas
a day…let’s say are you in line waiting

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.830
with a mask waiting to be let into a grocery
store and you are like “Wait…that lady

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:34.830
reminds me of this.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:35.830
That would make the perfect story.”

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:40.890
Jim: I probably come up, I’m not kidding,
I probably come up with at least 10 ideas

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:48.700
for films a week or more and I write them
down and some have legs.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.650
I have a system where I figure out if it’s
actually a story or a script.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.520
I come up with them all the time, literally
all the time.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:03.900
There used to be a joke pitch that my writing
partner and I used to do to lighten the room

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.670
when we would be in a meeting.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.930
We’d say Oh we’ve got this idea for this
movie.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:14.700
It’s POINT BREAK on motor bikes and the
cyclones are coming in or the tornados are

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.570
coming in and they have to rob the banks before
the tornadoes hit and this FBI agent has to

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:19.820
infiltrate the group.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.290
It was a joke idea.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:25.260
Somebody sold that exact same script for a
half a million dollars a few years later.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.990
No I don’t think they stole our idea or
anything (it’s just in the ether) but what

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:33.060
seemed like a joke idea, somebody else sold.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.090
So it’s like you have to have a lot of ideas
and you have to know what ideas are going

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.090
to work.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.340
I’ve worked on a few scripts that are great,
fantastic scripts and nobody wants to make

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.260
them because there is just this issue or that
issue or the producers think it’s too hard

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:51.190
or it won’t make its money back so it’s
a balance between the business side and the

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.190
creative side.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:57.280
Film Courage: When you do have a great idea
what is your process for at least putting

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.210
it somewhere that you can come back to and
how do you know when you want to take it further?

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:06.600
Jim: Usually I email them to myself so I have
all of these emails that half the time don’t

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:13.570
make sense - SECOND ACT: All on a plane, giant
fight.

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:15.120
Guy looses his wife.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.180
Hitman finds out his ex-wife is married to
somebody else.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.180
Things like that.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.280
So I have all of these weird random things
but once I really dig in I have a process

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.100
to figure out if something can be a script
and usually I figure out the entire first

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.440
act beat-by-beat-by-beat (the first 20 pages).

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.320
This happens, that happens, this happens.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.940
The first six or seven scenes.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.730
Then I know what happens in the second act
turn, kind of what shifts the movie or the

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.290
story and then how it ends.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:47.240
And if I can figure all that out then I know
I can pretty much write a good script.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:49.389
That’s what I usually do first.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:54.370
I don’t want to know the whole story beat-by-beat-by-beat
because I figure things out along the way

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:55.750
that I would have never thought of.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:02.640
Now there are other times where I have kind
of thought the whole thing out in bigger beats

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.690
and bigger sections but that’s usually what
I do, I know the first act, how it ends, what

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:10.880
happens in the middle, and then what happens
in the end and that’s it.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.180
Film Courage: So it starts as an idea, you
could be pumping gas somewhere and you go

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:20.030
and quickly email yourself this little log
line or whatever?

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:24.081
And then from there maybe on a weekend or
whenever you have “spare time,” you go

00:05:24.081 --> 00:05:26.690
and try to write the first 20 pages?

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:27.690
Jim: Yes.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:34.210
Usually the ideas that I can’t stop thinking
about are the ones I know are good, they keep

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:38.840
popping back in my head because there are
certain ideas you come up with and “Nah,”

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.150
you throw it away.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.120
It’s pretty good but not great.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:49.450
But the ones that keep reoccurring are the
ones that I feel okay I’ve got to work on

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.000
this, there is something here because it’s
stuck on my head.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.040
And also too ideas are a great testing ground
with people too.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:02.490
This is for more commercial type films but
if you pitch an idea in two or three sentences

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:06.900
to somebody (just your friends or whoever)
they kind of do this weird thing, this is

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.980
when you have a good idea not when they say
“That’s great” but when they think about

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:12.920
it for about three seconds and they go “Ooh,
that’s good."

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.360
That’s when you know it's actually connecting
with people.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.650
So if you have an idea and you say that to
ten people and they kind of all go “Ohh?”

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.310
Or they ask you about it again later, you
know it’s a good idea.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.050
Film Courage: [Referring back to an earlier
discussion in the interview] So if I tell

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:27.460
you I have a great script about my uncle,
he’s really cool and he has a pawn shop

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:32.080
in Hoboken (NJ) and he’s just a really funny
guy and he always know when some “fence”

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.480
is coming in to like…and I look at you go…what
are you going to tell me?

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.200
Jim: Hmmm…I don’t know.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.990
If you want to take the time to write that?

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:47.210
But no, it might be the greatest script ever
but that is one thing I’ve noticed too is

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:52.390
that the screenplays that get a lot of traction
and a lot of people’s interest are things

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:57.270
that can be described in two or three sentences
because a lot of times if you’re pitching

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:00.800
the idea or a creative executive reads it,
they have to go down the hall and pitch it

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.139
to their boss.

00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:08.430
So if it’s too complicated, it’s going
to get lost in the translation.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:11.710
We sold a TV show that everybody understood
the pitch in two sentences.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:17.280
I think that’s why it kept snowballing and
snowballing because it was like Okay great!

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.900
Everybody got it without reading the pilot.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:21.900
It was like Okay good.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:24.127
So those kind of things seem to really work
with people, at least seem to really work

00:07:24.127 --> 00:07:26.310
with people (at least with getting through
the front door).

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:31.210
If the script is bad, it's not going to go
anywhere but you have to make things digestible

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:36.770
for people who are working…you have to keep
in mind real producers are working 20-30 projects

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:40.340
in development, hearing pitches all day, talking
about things, working on films.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:45.540
If you give them so overly complicated idea
about your uncle in a pawn shop they are going

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:46.540
to be like “Wait, what did you say again?”

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.540
10 minutes later.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:48.540
So there is that.

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:49.540
Questions For The Viewers:

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:50.780
How many movie ideas do you have a week?

